        id: "int-sci-2-s6t3-content-3",
        type: "info",
        content: "**Practical Activity — Understanding a USB Charger**\\n\\nInstead of building a charger from scratch, you can safely explore how a charger works by examining a commercial phone charger.\\n\\n**Activity 6.3 — Understanding Your Phone Charger**\\n\\n1. Look at a phone charger and find the label showing:\\n   • Input: 100–240V AC, 50–60Hz\\n   • Output: 5V DC, 1A or 2A\\n\\n2. Use a multimeter to measure the output voltage of a phone charger:\\n   • Set the multimeter to DC voltage measurement\\n   • Connect the red probe to the positive terminal (centre of USB)\\n   • Connect the black probe to the negative terminal (outer contact)\\n   • Read the voltage — it should be close to 5V\\n\\n**Discuss:** Why is the output of a phone charger steady DC while the input is varying AC? What components inside the charger make this conversion possible?",
