        id: "int-sci-2-s8t1-content-1",
        type: "info",
        content: "**What are Indigenous Beverages?**\\n\\nIndigenous beverages are traditional drinks that are:\\n• Rooted in cultural history and traditions\\n• Made using locally sourced ingredients\\n• Prepared using traditional methods (fermentation, brewing, etc.)\\n• Often have nutritional or medicinal benefits\\n• Hold social, ceremonial, or religious significance\\n\\n**Examples of Indigenous Beverages in Ghana:**\\n\\n1. **Sobolo** — Hibiscus tea made from dried hibiscus flowers (zobo leaves), infused with ginger, pineapple, cloves, and other spices. Served chilled.\\n\\n2. **Asaana** — A non-alcoholic fermented drink made from caramelised corn. Known as \"African Coca-Cola.\"\\n\\n3. **Brukina** — A fermented drink made from cow milk and millet.\\n\\n4. **Pito** — A fermented alcoholic beverage made from sorghum or millet.\\n\\n5. **Palm Wine** — An alcoholic beverage obtained from the sap of palm trees, fermented by natural airborne yeasts.\\n\\n6. **Ginger Drink** — A refreshing drink made from fresh ginger, sugar, and spices.",
