        id: "int-sci-2-s8t3-content-3",
        type: "info",
        content: "**Quality Control and Preservation**\\n\\nTo ensure the quality and safety of indigenous beverages:\\n\\n**Hygiene Practices:**\\n• Wash hands thoroughly before handling ingredients\\n• Use clean utensils and containers\\n• Use clean, treated water\\n• Store finished beverages in clean, sealed containers\\n\\n**Quality Indicators:**\\n• **Colour:** Should be consistent with the expected colour of the beverage\\n• **Smell:** Should have a pleasant, characteristic aroma — no off-odours\\n• **Taste:** Should have the expected flavour — sweet, tangy, or refreshing\\n• **Clarity:** Should be free from visible impurities (unless traditionally unfiltered)\\n\\n**Preservation:**\\n• Refrigeration extends shelf life to 3–5 days\\n• Pasteurisation allows longer storage\\n• Avoid leaving beverages at room temperature for extended periods\\n\\n**Entrepreneurship Opportunity:**\\nIndigenous beverages can be produced at home and sold at local markets, schools, and events. With proper hygiene and quality control, they can become a successful small business.",
